Create a VPC named datacenter-vpc in region us-east-1 with any IPv4 CIDR block through terraform.

The Terraform working directory is /home/bob/terraform. Create the main.tf file (do not create a different .tf file) to accomplish this task.

Note: Right-click under the EXPLORER section in VS Code and select Open in Integrated Terminal to launch the terminal.

SOLUTION
To create the VPC named datacenter-vpc in us-east-1 using Terraform, you will need to write a configuration block for the aws_vpc resource. Since the task specifies an IPv4 CIDR block, you can use a standard private range like 10.0.0.0/16 .

Here is the content you should place in your /home/bob/terraform/main.tf file:

hcl
resource "aws_vpc" "datacenter_vpc" {
  cidr_block = "10.0.0.0/16"

  tags = {
    Name = "datacenter-vpc"
  }
}
💡 Key Details
Region: The task specifies us-east-1. In KodeKloud environments, the AWS provider is usually pre-configured for you, so you typically do not need to hardcode the provider block unless instructed otherwise.

CIDR Block: You can choose any valid IPv4 CIDR block (e.g., 10.0.0.0/16 or 192.168.0.0/24) as the task allows "any IPv4 CIDR block".

Name Tag: The Name tag inside the tags block is what ensures the VPC appears as datacenter-vpc in the AWS console .

🚀 Execution Steps
After creating the file, run the following commands in your terminal (inside /home/bob/terraform):

bash
terraform init
terraform plan
terraform apply --auto-approve
